# The same three things .gitignore spends a paragraph on, for the same reason and in the second
# place they can escape. A database carries every team's ViewCode in PLAINTEXT, deliberately, so
# a manager can re-share the join link, plus the roster of minors. An archive carries that AND
# the Data Protection key ring, which is the material that signs the access cookie: holding one
# is enough to forge access, not merely to read a copy.
#
# Harmless today only because neither Dockerfile does `COPY . .` — the canonical one copies
# global.json and HockeyPractice/, and Dockerfile.fast copies publish/. That is a property of
# two files that nobody is obliged to preserve. One future `COPY . .` would push this to a
# registry, and `restore/` currently holds two real databases.
restore/
*.db
hockeypractice-*.zip

# A framework-dependent publish ships SQLite's native library for every RID .NET knows about —
# Windows, macOS, Android, MIPS, s390x, the lot. That's 24 MB, of which this container can only
# ever load one: the base image is mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:10.0, which is Ubuntu 24.04 on
# amd64 (it was Debian 12 on 8.0), so linux-x64 (glibc) is the one it needs. NOT linux-musl-x64 —
# that's for Alpine. A side effect: an image built with --platform linux/arm64 for a quick native
# test on a Mac starts, then fails every query with a TypeInitializationException from
# SqliteConnection, because its library was stripped here. That is this rule, not a bug.
#
# Only affects Dockerfile.fast, which copies the host's publish/ output. The canonical Dockerfile
# publishes inside the build container, where this doesn't apply.
#
# Left in, this made the app layer ~45 MB and the registry began timing out the upload with a
# 524 partway through. Dropping the dead weight roughly halves both the image and the push.
publish/runtimes/**
!publish/runtimes/linux-x64/**
